  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nSurface high pressure stretched from the Canadian Prairies to \nthe Great Lakes early this afternoon. Mainly clear skies were\nobserved from the Iron Range northward with cloud cover moving\nin from the south. Dry conditions will prevail this afternoon\nand tonight with highs in the 40s and lower 50s and lows tonight\nfalling into the upper 20s to middle 30s.\n\nThe ridge axis will begin to shift to the east tonight with\nwarmer 850mb temps advecting northward. A weak warm front will\nlift from the Central Plains late tonight into southern South\nDakota and southern Minnesota by Saturday evening. Warm air\nadvection and isentropic lift with the front will lead to\nchances for showers through the day and into the evening.\nRainfall amounts will be light and dry air near the surface will\nlikely limit what reaches the ground. Highs will be in the 50s\nwith temperatures falling into the 40s and lower 50s after\nsunset. Sunday looks mainly dry outside of a few spotty showers\nas temperatures continue to warm. Highs will be in the middle\n50s to middle 60s with lows in the 40s.\n\nThe pattern turns more active heading into the new week with a\nlarge system impacting the central CONUS in the Monday-Tuesday\ntimeframe. A deep trough will dig into the Rockies Sunday night\ninto Monday with surface low pressure developing across eastern\nColorado. This low moves northeast through the day Monday and\ninto the Upper Midwest Monday night. Widespread rainfall will\nspread across the Northland through the day Monday and into\nMonday night. Heading into Tuesday, an upper low will drop out\nof the Canadian Prairies and interact with the system over the\nUpper Midwest. Questions remain as to what will happen during\nthe interaction, but what is known is that rainfall will\ncontinue for Tuesday into Tuesday night as the system slowly\nmoves through the region. PWATs with this system will be in\nexcess of an inch and approach an inch and a quarter, which\nwould be over the 90th percentile for the date and near the\ndaily max. Widespread rainfall amounts of three-quarters of inch\nto 1.25 inches is expected with an axis of higher amounts in\nexcess of 2 inches. This heaviest axis of rainfall has been\nwobbling across the region from model run to run, so placement\nis uncertain at this time. However, the signal for it remains\nstrong. Highs Monday and Tuesday will be in the 50s and 60s.\n\nChances for showers will linger for Wednesday as this system\ndeparts with drier conditions for the latter half of the week as\nhigh pressure builds in. Cooler air will filter in behind the\nsystem with highs retreating into the 40s and 50s for Thursday\nand Friday.",
